- Opponent blocks Rallier: both die (Rallier 2/2 takes 3, deals 2 to 1-toughness). Jailer through for 3. Opponent at 15.\n\
- No block: 5 damage, opponent at 13.\n\
\n\
Opponent will likely trade Ostrich-Horse with Jailer (my biggest threat). Hmm, but is that good for them? They lose their only creature. Actually with 7 cards in hand they probably have more plays.\n\
\n\
Alternatively, cast Cat-Owl first, then attack. If I cast Cat-Owl precombat, opponent knows I have a flyer. Doesn't change their block much.\n\
\n\
Actually, the standard play: attack first, then cast postcombat, to give opponent less information. But here, if I attack and they trade Ostrich-Horse for Jailer, then I cast Cat-Owl. That's fine.\n\
\n\
Should I hold Airbending Lesson? It's removal for later — it can hit any nonland permanent. With Swampsnare Trap in their graveyard, they have removal too. I'd hold Airbending Lesson for a bigger threat, or use it to save... no, it targets any nonland permanent — could be used on my own? \"Airbend target nonland permanent\" — no target restriction to opponents'. Could save my own creature from removal and recast for {2}. Hmm, Jailer costs 3 presumably ({2}{W}?). Airbending my own Jailer would let me recast for {2} and re-trigger ETB. Interesting but situational.\n\
\n\
Decision: Play Island, attack with both Rallier and Jailer, then cast Cat-Owl postcombat.\n\
\n\
Wait, should I attack with Jailer at all? If they trade Ostrich-Horse for it... Actually let's think: is trading my 3/3 Jailer for their 3/1 Ostrich-Horse good? Ostrich-Horse already did its ETB (milled 3). It's just a 3/1 now. Jailer is a 3/3 with a useful ETB that's already used. On board, 3/3 > 3/1. But they only have one creature; if they trade, they're left with nothing and I have Rallier + Cat-Owl. That's favorable for me. They might not block and just take 5, then play their own stuff. Either way attacking is right.\n\
\n\
Could opponent have removal for my attackers? They have 7 cards. Swampsnare Trap is in graveyard. They could have another. Risk exists but attacking is still correct.\n\
\n\
Actually, one more consideration: should I cast Cat-Owl precombat to potentially have it as a blocker? No, it'd be sick. Can't block... wait, sick creatures CAN block. Summoning sickness prevents attacking only (and tap abilities). So Cat-Owl cast this turn can block next turn regardless.\n\
\n\
Plan: Play Island. Attack with Rallier + Jailer. Then cast Cat-Owl postcombat (2 Islands + Plains + ... Cat-Owl {3}{W/U} = 4 mana; I have 4 lands after playing Island).\n\
\n\
Actually, order: I should play the Island now (precombat), pass to combat, attack, then postcombat cast Cat-Owl.\n\
\n\
Wait, can I do anything better with mana? Precombat cast Cat-Owl vs postcombat — no difference except information. Postcombat is slightly better (opponent doesn't know about flyer when deciding blocks... actually they might block differently if they know I have a flyer coming? Minimal). Postcombat is standard.\n\
\n\
