Benevolent River Spirit: UU with waterbend 5 additional cost - that's a lot. It effectively costs 7 mana-ish worth of tapping. Late-game flying threat with ward. My deck has waterbend payoffs but paying waterbend 5 requires having creatures/artifacts to tap. Actually in this set, \"waterbend N\" as a cost means \"You may tap N untapped artifacts and/or creatures you control rather than pay this spell's/generic mana cost\" - hmm, let me recall. Avatar set mechanics: Waterbend {X}: You may tap X untapped artifacts and/or creatures you control rather than pay this generic mana cost. Actually the reminder: \"Waterbend is an ability that lets you tap creatures and artifacts to pay for costs. Each artifact or creature tapped this way pays for {1}.\"\n\
\n\
So Waterbend {5} means tap 5 things (creatures/artifacts) to pay the {5} generic part? For Crashing Wave it's \"as an additional cost to cast, waterbend {X}\" - tap X creatures/artifacts.\n\
\n\
For Benevolent River Spirit: {U}{U} plus waterbend {5} - that's expensive: 2 blue mana + tap 5 things. That's a big ask mid-game. Late game it's a flier with ward 2 and scry 2. Reasonable but clunky.\n\
\n\
Airbender's Reversal: cheap instant, destroy attacking creature - solid removal, or flicker-ish effect (airbend = exile and return? Airbend: \"Exile it. Until... \" Actually airbend returns the card? Airbend target nonland permanent - from Airbending Lesson: \"Airbend target nonland permanent. Draw a card.\" Airbend likely means exile and return to battlefield tapped? Or return to owner's hand? In the Avatar set, airbend = \"Exile it. For as long as it remains exiled, its owner may cast it\" no... Let me think. Airbending in the set: \"Airbend [something]\" means exile it, and its owner may cast it for {2} less? Something like that. Aang, Swift Savior: \"airbend up to one other target creature or spell.\" \n\
\n\
Regardless, Airbender's Reversal is flexible removal for 2 mana. Destroy target attacking creature is always useful.\n\
\n\
The Legend of Kuruk gives card selection and draw - chapters I and II each scry 2 and draw, then transform into a creature presumably strong. In a deck that wants Lessons and card advantage, this mythic is great. Also my deck has double blue support (10+ blue sources).\n\
\n\
Given my deck could use a strong finisher/card engine, Kuruk is the pick. It's a mythic bomb.\n\
\n\
Alternatively Airbender's Reversal is cheap removal which my deck has some of already (Honest Work, Jailer x2, Watery Grasp, Crashing Wave, Rowdy Snowballers). Removal is covered. Card advantage: Waterbending Lesson x2, Agna Qel'a. Kuruk adds a lot.\n\
\n\
Flexible Waterbender is a mediocre common.\n\
\n\
I'll pick The Legend of Kuruk (#13).\n\
\n\
Wait, but should I consider signaling/wheeling? It's pick 2 of pack 3; not much wheeling concern. Take the strongest on-color card: Kuruk.\n\
\n\
Double-check: is Kuruk's transformed side good? \"Exile this Saga, then return it to the battlefield transformed.\" Kuruk transformed is likely a creature (Kuruk, something). Even without knowing, two scry-2-draws plus a creature is worth 2UU. Good.\n\
\n\
